        "titulo": "Gern, lieber, am liebsten: what I like and prefer",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «gern» (gladly) goes AFTER the verb and says you like doing something: «Ich schwimme gern» — \"I swim gladly\" = I like swimming. German doesn't need a separate \"like\" verb here. STEP 2: «nicht gern» says you don't like it: «Ich koche nicht gern» (I don't like cooking). STEP 3: «lieber» says what you PREFER: «Ich lese lieber» (I'd rather read). STEP 4: «am liebsten» says what you like MOST: «Am liebsten tanze ich» (what I like best is dancing). The scale is: gern → lieber → am liebsten — like good → better → best, but for liking. STEP 5: useful questions: «Was machst du gern?» (What do you like doing?), «Was machst du am Wochenende?», «Was ist dein Hobby?» STEP 6: «Mein Hobby ist Fußball» (one) or «Meine Hobbys sind Lesen und Tanzen» (several).",
        "comparacao": "English says \"I like swimming\" with a like-verb plus -ing. German just adds «gern» to the verb you do: «Ich schwimme gern». And «lieber» is the old English \"liefer\" (\"I'd as lief…\"), meaning \"rather\" — German kept it.",
